# Line endings, and the files whose bytes are checksummed.
#
# This file is a prerequisite for prismabib working on Windows at all, not a
# tidiness measure. Git for Windows installs with `core.autocrlf=true`, so
# without an explicit policy every text file in a Windows clone arrives with
# CRLF line endings -- including the reference fixture's captured Scopus pages,
# whose bytes are covered by `manifest.json`'s `payload_sha256`. A CRLF
# checkout changes those bytes, the checksum no longer matches, and the whole
# Layer 0 -> Layer 1 chain fails on a machine where nothing is actually wrong.

# Normalise to LF in the repository and check out LF everywhere. `text=auto`
# still lets git detect and leave binary content alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Byte-exact fixtures: never normalised, in either direction.
#
# `-text` is not merely "LF here too". It tells git the content is opaque: no
# normalisation on commit, no conversion on checkout, and no per-user
# `core.autocrlf` override. These files are provenance, not source -- the
# sealed Layer 0 pages and their manifest carry a SHA-256 over their exact
# bytes (BUILD_PLAN §2.2), and the recorded API payloads under `cassettes/`
# exist to be replayed verbatim.
tests/fixtures/projects/reference/raw/** -text !eol
tests/fixtures/cassettes/*.json -text !eol

# The decision log and its sidecar, wherever a project directory is committed
# (`track_decisions = true`, BUILD_PLAN §2.5). `decisions.jsonl` is append-only,
# fsynced, and covered by a `sha256sum`-compatible sidecar; a checkout that
# rewrote its newlines would make every line's checksum wrong and read, to
# `DecisionLog.load`, as tampering.
decisions.jsonl -text !eol
decisions.jsonl.sha256 -text !eol
